      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After the 1680 Pueblo Revolt, Spanish governors issued the 1689 Picuris Pueblo Grant, covering 17,000 acres.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rio_lucio_new_mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Río Lucio emerged as a small Hispano settlement in the mid‑19th century during New Mexico’s transition to U.S. rule.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rio_lucio_new_mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A nearby spring allowed farmers to grow corn, chili, and raise sheep and goats, sharing resources with Picuris Pueblo.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
